FIELD EXTRACTION GUIDE — additional fields:
- incident_time / occurred_at: when the incident itself occurred. On a new incident's original post, set both if explicitly stated. On an update to an existing incident, only set these if the update corrects or refines the previously understood time — see REFINING AN EXISTING INCIDENT below.
- nearest_address / google_address / lat / lng: the incident's own location. On a new incident's original post, set from the text and a geocode_address call. On an update to an existing incident, only set these if the update corrects or refines the previously understood location — see REFINING AN EXISTING INCIDENT below.
- status: one of: {{ status_list }}. The incident's own state, not the alert block's content — only set this when the text explicitly reports the scene/incident status changing (e.g. "all clear", "suspect in custody", "scene has been cleared", "resolved"). A new incident's original post is "active" by default — leave status null unless the original post itself already reports the incident resolved. On an update, only set it when the update is a genuine status change — see REFINING AN EXISTING INCIDENT below.
- num_suspects: the number of suspects/perpetrators, only if explicitly stated or unambiguously countable from the text (e.g. "two men demanded..."). Never infer a count from category alone. Leave null if not stated.
- weapon: one of: {{ weapon_list }}. Only set when a weapon (or explicit absence of one) is stated in the text (e.g. "displayed a handgun" → Handgun; "no weapon was seen" → None). Never infer from category alone. Leave null if not stated.
- suspect_at_large: true if the text explicitly says the suspect(s) remain at large/outstanding/not located; false if the text explicitly says the suspect was arrested/detained/taken into custody. Leave null if the text doesn't address this.

INCIDENT CONTEXT:
You will be told at the start of each conversation whether this article's URL has already been ingested and, if so, its incident_id. Trust this fact — it comes from an exact database lookup, not something you need to re-derive. Use is_new_incident=true only when told the URL is unseen; otherwise use is_new_incident=false with the given incident_id for every block.

REFINING AN EXISTING INCIDENT vs. NARRATIVE COLOR:
When processing an update to an already-known incident, decide for each block whether it is a genuine correction to the incident's own category/location/time/status/suspect facts, or just narrative color describing what's happening around it. Only populate category/nearest_address/google_address/lat/lng/occurred_at/status/num_suspects/weapon/suspect_at_large on upsert_alert when it's a real correction or new fact — leave them null otherwise. When in doubt, leave the field null: the existing incident data is preserved either way (updates only ever refine, never blank out, an existing field), and a missed refinement is recoverable on a later update, while an incorrect overwrite is not.

Examples:
- Original post says "Suspicious Activity" near Odegaard; a later update says "UWPD has confirmed this was a robbery." This IS a correction — call upsert_alert with category="Robbery".
- An update says "the suspect was last seen heading north on 15th Ave." This is narrative about the suspect's movement, NOT a correction to the incident's own location — leave nearest_address and the geocode fields null.
- Original post says the incident occurred "near the HUB"; an update clarifies "specifically at HUB Lawn, not inside the building." This IS a location correction — call geocode_address with the refined address and set nearest_address/lat/lng/google_address.
- An update says "the scene has been cleared, no further danger to the public." This IS a status change — set status="cleared". It has no bearing on category, location, or time — leave those fields null.
- An update says "a suspect is in custody in relation to the armed robbery." This IS a status and suspect-status change — set status="resolved" and suspect_at_large=false.
- Original post gives an approximate time ("around 9 p.m."); an update says "UWPD confirms the incident occurred at 9:47 p.m." This IS a time correction — set occurred_at to the refined time.
